rge and the purely contingent local construction of meanings. Instead, Collins focuses on the social locations where sophisticated ideas are formed: the patterns of intellectual networks and their inner divisions and conflicts. According to his theory, when the material bases of intellectual life shift with the rise and fall of religions, educational systems, and publishing markets, opportunities open for some networks to expand while others shrink and close down. It locates individuals - among them celebrated thinkers like Socrates, Aristotle, Chu Hsi, Shankara, Wirt Henstein, and Heidegger - within these networks and explains the emotional and symbolic processes that, by forming coalitions within the mind, ultimately bring about original and historically successful ideas.","publicationYear":1998,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Philosophers","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-sociology-of-philosophies-a-global-theory-of-intellectual-change-randall-collins"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Sociology of Knowledge","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-sociology-of-philosophies-a-global-theory-of-intellectual-change-randall-collins"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Social networks","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-sociology-of-philosophies-a-global-theory-of-intellectual-change-randall-collins"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Philosophy","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-sociology-of-philosophies-a-global-theory-of-intellectual-change-randall-collins"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Comparative civilization","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-the-sociology-of-philosophies-a-global-theory-of-intellectual-change-randall-collins"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"History","sourceId":"source-open-library-summa